<p>Good questions do two things: they tell you what to offer, and they help the other person see their own need more clearly. Ask open questions (what, how, tell me about) and follow the interesting answers with “tell me more”.</p>

<h2>The situation</h2>
<ul>
  <li>What made you get in touch now?</li>
  <li>Tell me a bit about how things are today.</li>
</ul>

<h2>The problem and what it costs them</h2>
<ul>
  <li>What’s not working the way you’d like?</li>
  <li>What happens if nothing changes?</li>
  <li>Have you tried anything already? How did it go?</li>
</ul>

<h2>What good looks like</h2>
<ul>
  <li>If this goes really well, what’s different in three months?</li>
  <li>What matters most to you in choosing who to work with?</li>
</ul>

<h2>Timing, decision and budget</h2>
<ul>
  <li>When would you like this done by? Is anything driving that date?</li>
  <li>Who else is involved in deciding?</li>
  <li>Do you have a budget in mind, even a rough range? (It helps me suggest the right option.)</li>
</ul>

<h2>Habits that help</h2>
<ul>
  <li>Ask one question at a time, then stop talking. Silence is fine.</li>
  <li>Write their words down; use them back in your recap and quote.</li>
  <li>Don’t solve too early. The third answer to “tell me more” is usually the real reason.</li>
</ul>
